# Single entry point, fronted by Traefik in this deployment.
#   SITE_ADDRESS=:80  -> plain HTTP, match any Host (Traefik terminates routing/TLS)
# Traefik routes browser42.localhost (and BROWSER42_BASE_URL) to this Caddy on :80.
#
# THE GATE ON THE LIVE VIEW AND THE HAND-OFF (DGO-988).
# `/view/*` (the live screen), `/websockify` (its keyboard and mouse) and `/handoff/*` (wakes the
# browser and brings a tab to the front) were protected by NOTHING here: the only check was the
# Authentik forwardAuth on Traefik's routers, and that check exists only on the requests that
# traverse a router the edge rendered. Every container on the shared network (`traefik-<deployment>`)
# reaches this Caddy by its service name and skips the edge entirely — so any container, including a
# compromised one, had a keyboard and a mouse on the office's browser, which is where the owner's
# SSO sessions live. This Caddy is the app's ONE door, so the app checks its own callers.
#
# WHAT IT ASKS, AND WHY THIS IS AUTHENTICATION RATHER THAN A HEADER: it repeats the edge's own call —
# the identity provider's forward-auth endpoint, with THIS REQUEST'S OWN COOKIE — and lets the
# request through only when the provider answers 2xx. A cookie cannot be invented by a container on
# the network (it is signed by the provider); an `X-authentik-*` header can, by any container, so the
# headers are neither trusted nor copied onwards. This is the same trust model as
# `apps/project-pln/office_identity.py` ("never X-authentik-* on the request — any container can
# write it"). No shared secret is involved, so there is no key for a container to find: the app
# holds no credential of its own for this door, only the address of the provider.
#
# WHICH provider is the office's one switch, `DGO_IDENTITY` (.env) — the value the edge's own
# middleware reads (platform-base/traefik/config/authentik-forwardauth.yaml) — so this door asks
# whoever the edge asks (r030-002). It is resolved when Caddy reads this file: a value with no
# snippet below is a Caddyfile that does not load, so the door stays shut rather than open.
(gate) {
	import gate-{$DGO_IDENTITY:authentik}
}

(gate-authentik) {
	forward_auth http://authentik-server:9000 {
		uri /outpost.goauthentik.io/auth/traefik
		import gate-headers
	}
}

(gate-forward-auth) {
	forward_auth http://forward-auth:9078 {
		uri /auth/traefik
		import gate-headers
	}
}

(gate-headers) {
	# `X-Forwarded-Host` is the app's host as the EDGE saw it, and the provider matches the
	# application by it: `console.<domain>` for a request that arrives through the console's own
	# proxy (which drops `Host` but forwards this header) and for the passthrough routers, and
	# `browser42.<domain>` for this app's own routers. Caddy would otherwise overwrite it with this
	# request's `Host` (`browser42-caddy:80` behind the console), which matches no application and
	# would refuse a signed-in owner. A request with no such header — a container calling this Caddy
	# by its service name — is refused, which is exactly the caller this exists to stop.
	header_up X-Forwarded-Host {http.request.header.X-Forwarded-Host}
	header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {http.request.header.X-Forwarded-Proto}
	header_up X-Forwarded-Uri {http.request.uri}
	header_up X-Forwarded-Method {http.request.method}
	header_up -Connection
	header_up -Upgrade
}

{$SITE_ADDRESS::80} {
	# No basic-auth here: access control is done declaratively at the Traefik
	# edge (Authentik forward-auth on the -https and -local routers, like machine
	# / homepage) AND, since DGO-988, at the three doors below (the `gate` snippet),
	# because the edge's check does not exist for a caller that does not cross it.
	# The noVNC view's VNC password is an OPTIONAL extra factor
	# (BROWSER42_VNC_PASSWORD); empty by default, and it is not a gate: /view/ writes
	# it into its own refresh URL, so anyone who can read the view has it.

	# Live browser view (noVNC): open /view/ and you SEE the agent's browser.
	# The console injects <base href="/a/browser42/"> into this HTML, so the relative
	# websocket path below resolves to /a/browser42/websockify when embedded and to
	# /websockify when standalone. Including the mount prefix here would apply it twice.
	# `{query}` carries the hand-off's honest marker (`/view/?lost=<slug>` — the tab it promised could
	# not be brought to the front) through to the viewer, which says so (browser-navigation.js).
	# The redirects below carry no browser state and are gated nothing; the FILES are, and they are
	# what draws the live screen.
	redir /view /view/vnc.html?autoconnect=true&reconnect=true&resize=remote&quality=6&compression=2&{query}
	redir /view/ /view/vnc.html?autoconnect=true&reconnect=true&resize=remote&quality=6&compression=2&path=websockify&{query}
	handle_path /view/* {
		import gate
		# The viewer is CODE THAT CHANGES: noVNC's files are patched at container start by
		# start-browser.sh 4b, which injects browser42-navigation/-clipboard/-keyboard into vnc.html.
		# Python's static handler — what websockify serves with — sends only Last-Modified, and a
		# browser keeps serving the copy it already has, so a deployed fix never reached the owner's
		# phone: measured 2026-10-14 on DGO-575, the injected <script> tag was served while the phone
		# still ran the page from before it. `no-cache` means REVALIDATE, never "don't store": with
		# Last-Modified the answer is a 304, and no fix depends on a human reloading by hand.
		header Cache-Control "no-cache"
		reverse_proxy {$BROWSER42_UPSTREAM}:6080
	}
	# noVNC opens its websocket back to /websockify on the same origin. This is a keyboard and a
	# mouse on the shared browser, so it is gated like the screen it belongs to; a websocket
	# handshake carries the same cookie, and the gate's auth subrequest drops `Connection`/`Upgrade`
	# so the provider is asked a plain GET and the tunnel is left to the proxy below.
	handle /websockify {
		import gate
		reverse_proxy {$BROWSER42_UPSTREAM}:6080
	}
	# MCP endpoint: point your MCP client at <addr>/mcp with header
	#   Authorization: Bearer $BROWSER42_MCP_TOKEN
	# Authentik (browser SSO) can't gate non-browser MCP clients, so the bearer
	# token is enforced here in Caddy — it applies on every ingress (the public
	# Traefik route AND the loopback dev port). @playwright/mcp has DNS-rebinding
	# protection and 403s unless the Host header is its bind address, so rewrite it.
	# A caller with no browser has no cookie to be identified by, so this door keeps its bearer
	# token and is deliberately NOT behind `gate` (DGO-988 leaves it exactly as it was).
	handle_path /mcp* {
		@unauthorized not header Authorization "Bearer {$MCP_TOKEN}"
		route {
			respond @unauthorized "Unauthorized" 401 {
				close
			}
			reverse_proxy browser42-mcp:8931 {
				header_up Host localhost:8931
			}
		}
	}

	# The human hand-off (DGO-571): /handoff/<slug> wakes the browser, brings that group's tab to the
	# front and lands on /view/ — apps/browser42/handoff.py, started by start-browser.sh §4f-bis. Behind
	# the console it arrives through the /a/browser42/handoff passthrough (app.json), prefix stripped;
	# the route reads X-Forwarded-Prefix to land back inside the mount.
	# The token it carries is what handoff.py requires (a hand-off wakes the browser and moves tabs),
	# and that token is Caddy's own — handed to every caller that gets this far. So this door is gated
	# FIRST, for the same reason as the view (DGO-988): a hand-off is a pointer at a signed-in tab.
	handle /handoff/* {
		import gate
		reverse_proxy {$BROWSER42_UPSTREAM}:8767 {
			header_up X-Browser42-Handoff {$MCP_TOKEN}
		}
	}

	# Bare / -> the live view.
	redir / /view/

	# Nothing else is this app's door — say so.
	# Caddy's default for "no route matched" is an EMPTY 200, and an empty 200 reads as success to
	# every caller that looks at the status alone: measured 2026-10-14, `POST /v1/task` through the
	# link's door answered 200 with length 0 while browser42's task runner (8766) was not published
	# anywhere, so a linked office's agent could not tell "no door" from "answered nothing"
	# (lnk-037/DGO-570). A 404 is the one answer that cannot be mistaken for one.
	handle {
		respond 404
	}
}

# The agent door (DGO-32, lnk-037, DGO-1002): a linked office's `bbox b42 task --office` posts /v1/task to
# <alias>.<office>:<agent_api_port>, and the link delivers to the app's ROUTED service, this Caddy, on that
# port. Two numbers name that port, and this site listens on both:
#   8766 — the one app.json states on the release line (45a69657, DGO-570);
#   8931 — what a line that still derives it from the MCP capability publishes. The HP's link served
#          browser42-caddy:8931 to the Mac, the Dell and vps1 with nothing listening here, and every far
#          `b42 task` got 502 (measured 2026-10-22 from the Mac and inside dgo-link-hp). The host's own
#          127.0.0.1:8931 is this container's :80, the site above, so nothing here collides with it.
# The task runner is a container of its own (8766): this site hands it that one path and nothing else; the
# screen (/view), the hand-off and the MCP stay on the site above. The design is the Mac session's (FPG-29).
# The runner asks no credential and already sits on the shared network, so this door reaches nothing a
# container there could not; through the link every office of ours drives every verb, `login` included —
# the owner's decision: one browser, in the HP, that all our offices use (FPG-29, DGO-570).
:8766, :8931 {
	handle /v1/task {
		reverse_proxy browser42-task-runner:8766
	}
	respond 404
}
